`./simplify 1 --convex` crashes because `find_tangent_idx()` sometimes returns **not exactly 2 tangent vertices**, but `find_F()` assumes it always does and asserts.

The failing path is here:

```612:618:/Users/sinchunyeung/UROP1100Q/simplify.cpp
std::vector<Point> find_F(const Point& p, const std::vector<Point>& S) {
    if (S.size() == 1 || point_in_convex(p, S)) {
        return current_bbox();
    }
    std::vector<int> tangent = find_tangent_idx(p, S);
    assert(tangent.size() == 2);
    std::optional<Point> hit1 = intersect_ray_with_rect(p, S[tangent[0]]);
```

And the tangent detection is this:

```567:582:/Users/sinchunyeung/UROP1100Q/simplify.cpp
std::vector<int> find_tangent_idx(const Point &p,
                                 const std::vector<Point> &S) {
    int n = S.size();
    std::vector<int> tangent;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        Point pred = S[(i - 1 + n) % n];
        Point now = S[i];
        Point succ = S[(i + 1 + n) % n];
        if (CGAL::orientation(p, pred, now)
                != CGAL::orientation(p, now, succ) &&
            CGAL::orientation(p, now, succ) != CGAL::COLLINEAR) {
            tangent.push_back(i);
        }
    }
    return tangent;
}
```

## Why this breaks

That heuristic is not robust. It can fail when:

- `S` has **collinear consecutive vertices**
- `S` is **degenerate / nearly degenerate**
- `p` lies in a position where one tangent touches a **flat chain**
- the polygon from the previous convex intersection is not in the strict form this logic expects

In those cases, the orientation-change test may produce:

- `0` tangents
- `1` tangent
- `>2` tangents

and then this assert fires:

```617:617:/Users/sinchunyeung/UROP1100Q/simplify.cpp
assert(tangent.size() == 2);
```

## Why this specifically happens with `--convex`

Your separate test already showed the custom convex intersection is often wrong. So with `--convex`, `S[i]` is being updated by:

```740:746:/Users/sinchunyeung/UROP1100Q/simplify.cpp
std::vector<Point> inter_result;
if (use_convex_intersection) {
    auto convex_start = Clock::now();
    inter_result = convex_polygon_intersection(F, Gi);
    g_timing.convex_intersection += Clock::now() - convex_start;
    ++g_timing.convex_intersection_calls;
```

That means `S[i]` may become:

- non-convex in practice
- self-ordered incorrectly
- full of duplicate/collinear vertices
- missing vertices it should have

Then later `find_F(p0, S[i])` calls `find_tangent_idx(p, S)`, which assumes `S` is a nice convex polygon. That assumption is violated, so the tangent count is no longer 2.

## So the root cause is

Not the assert itself.  
The assert is catching a real invariant violation:

- `find_F()` requires `S` to admit exactly 2 tangents from `p`
- under `--convex`, the custom `convex_polygon_intersection()` often produces bad `S`
- then `find_tangent_idx()` fails on that bad `S`

## Most likely chain of failure

1. `--convex` enables `convex_polygon_intersection`
2. that function returns an incorrect polygon
3. incorrect polygon is stored in `S[i]`
4. later `find_F(p0, S[i])` runs
5. `find_tangent_idx(p0, S[i])` returns size != 2
6. assert fires

## Evidence from your earlier run

Your `separate.cpp` comparison against CGAL showed the custom convex intersection only passed about 21% of tests, including failures on simple overlaps like:
- overlapping squares
- triangle-triangle overlap
- partial edge intersections
- many random convex cases

So `--convex` is feeding invalid geometry downstream.

## Short answer

`./simplify 1 --convex` crashes because the custom convex intersection routine corrupts `S`, and then `find_tangent_idx()` can no longer find exactly two tangents, so `assert(tangent.size() == 2)` aborts.

If you want, I can next pinpoint the exact bad polygon instance for `./simplify 1 --convex` by adding targeted debug prints right before the assert, without removing the assert.

`./simplify 1 --convex` crashes because `find_tangent_idx()` returns a vector whose size is **not 2**, and `find_F()` asserts that it must be 2.

The crash site is:

```612:618:/Users/sinchunyeung/UROP1100Q/simplify.cpp
std::vector<Point> find_F(const Point& p, const std::vector<Point>& S) {
    if (S.size() == 1 || point_in_convex(p, S)) {
        return current_bbox();
    }
    std::vector<int> tangent = find_tangent_idx(p, S);
    assert(tangent.size() == 2);
```

The tangent finder is:

```567:582:/Users/sinchunyeung/UROP1100Q/simplify.cpp
std::vector<int> find_tangent_idx(const Point &p,
                                 const std::vector<Point> &S) {
    int n = S.size();
    std::vector<int> tangent;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        Point pred = S[(i - 1 + n) % n];
        Point now = S[i];
        Point succ = S[(i + 1 + n) % n];
        if (CGAL::orientation(p, pred, now)
                != CGAL::orientation(p, now, succ) &&
            CGAL::orientation(p, now, succ) != CGAL::COLLINEAR) {
            tangent.push_back(i);
        }
    }
    return tangent;
}
```

Why it happens under `--convex`:

- `--convex` switches intersection updates to `convex_polygon_intersection(...)`
- your standalone testing already showed that routine is often wrong
- that bad output gets stored into `S[i]`
- later `find_F(p0, S[i])` assumes `S[i]` is a valid convex polygon with exactly 2 tangents from `p0`
- that invariant is broken, so `find_tangent_idx()` returns 0, 1, or more than 2 entries
- the assert correctly catches it

The relevant update path is:

```740:746:/Users/sinchunyeung/UROP1100Q/simplify.cpp
std::vector<Point> inter_result;
if (use_convex_intersection) {
    auto convex_start = Clock::now();
    inter_result = convex_polygon_intersection(F, Gi);
    g_timing.convex_intersection += Clock::now() - convex_start;
    ++g_timing.convex_intersection_calls;
```

So the real reason is:

- **not** “the assert is too strict”
- **yes** “the custom convex intersection produces an invalid `S` for downstream tangent logic”

Most likely failure modes of that bad `S`:
- wrong vertex order
- duplicate vertices
- collinear chains
- missing vertices
- non-convex result despite expecting convex

That matches your earlier `separate.cpp` result: the custom convex intersection failed many simple and random convex cases, so it is not reliable enough to preserve the invariant required by `find_F()`.

